narrative:
## The L2 Data Toolkit

You now have a complete toolbox:

| Concept | Syntax | Use For |
|---------|--------|---------|
| Struct | `type Name = { ... }` | Grouping related fields |
| Enum | `enum Name { ... }` | One type, multiple choices |
| Method | `fn Type.method(self) { }` | Behavior attached to data |
| Nested struct | Field of another struct type | Hierarchical data |
| Enum with data | `Variant(field: Type)` | Choices that carry info |
| Match | `match val { V => { } }` | Branch on enum variants |

### Showcase: Library System

```xiom
type Book = {
  title: Str;
  author: Str;
  pages: Int;
}

enum BookStatus {
  Available,
  CheckedOut(due: Str),
  Lost,
}

fn Book.status(self) -> Str {
  return title + " by " + author + " (" + pages + " pages)";
}
```

### Showcase: Game Character

```xiom
type Stats = {
  strength: Int;
  agility: Int;
  intelligence: Int;
}

type Hero = {
  name: Str;
  stats: Stats;
  level: Int;
}

fn Hero.power_level(self) -> Int {
  return (stats.strength + stats.agility + stats.intelligence) * level;
}
```

### Showcase: Smart Home

```xiom
enum DeviceState {
  On,
  Off,
  Standby,
}

type Device = {
  name: Str;
  state: DeviceState;
  watts: Int;
}

fn Device.energy_usage(self) -> Int {
  match state {
    On => { return watts; }
    Standby => { return watts / 10; }
    Off => { return 0; }
  }
}
```

### What's Next

In L3, you'll add **collections** (lists, maps), **traits** (shared behavior), and **generics** (types that work with any data). But first: practice. Model something from your own life using structs and enums.

